.TH NAP 3 LOCAL
.SH NAME
nap, naphz \- sleep for a fraction of a second
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <nap.h>
.PP
.B nap(ticks)
.PP
.B naphz()
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Nap
suspends the calling process for
.I ticks
sixtieths of a second.
.I Naphz
returns the number of nap ticks in one second
.RB ( NAPHZ ,
60 in this version); divide by it to convert, and do not assume the
value.
On UTS a nap tick is the driver's own unit, timed from the S/370 TOD
clock: the kernel's
.I HZ
is 1 and has nothing to do with it.
.PP
Both open
.B /dev/nap
on first use and keep the descriptor.  The descriptor has the
close-on-exec bit set, so it is not inherited across
.IR exec .
.SH "RETURN VALUE"
Zero on success, \-1 with
.I errno
set on error.
.SH ERRORS
.TP
.SM ENXIO
.B /dev/nap
is missing, or the driver is not in the running kernel.
.TP
.SM EINVAL
.I ticks
is negative or exceeds
.SM NAPMAXT
(two seconds' worth).  Use
.IR sleep (3)
for longer delays.
.TP
.SM EAGAIN
Too many processes are napping at once (see
.SM NNAP
in the driver).  Retry.
.TP
.SM EMFILE
No descriptor was available for
.BR /dev/nap .
.SH "SEE ALSO"
sleep(3), alarm(2), time(2) for
.IR ftime ,
which measures sub-second time but cannot wait for it
.SH BUGS
A nap cannot be interrupted by a signal; the signal is delivered when
the nap ends.  This keeps the driver from ever being left mid-nap by a
non-local jump out of
.IR sleep (),
and is why the maximum nap is short.
.PP
The deadline is computed from the TOD clock at the moment of the call,
so a nap is never shorter than
.I ticks
ticks.  It may be longer: under VM/370 the clock comparator interrupt
that ends it is simulated by CP, and the UTS virtual machine must then
be dispatched before the napper runs.
